   260	\section{Models}
   261	
   262	\subsection{CSPs arising from satisfiability problems}\label{sec:satmodel}
   263	We first describe a class of constraint satisfaction problems.
   264	Let $V = \{v_1,\dots,v_n\}$ be a set of Boolean variables, and fix an integer $k\geq 2$.
   265	An instance $F$ of a CSP consists of a $k$-uniform multi-hypergraph $(V,E)$ (that is, all edges have cardinality $k$ and we allow parallel edges),
   266	% \footnote{The elements of $V$ are called the variables and members of $E$, called edges, are $k$-subsets of $V$. 
   267	% We allow multiple copies of the same edge.}
   268	and a family of \emph{clause functions} $\chi_e: \{0,1\}^k \rightarrow \{0,1\}$ for each $e\in E$.
   269	A \emph{$k$-clause} comprises an edge $e$ and its corresponding function $\chi_e$.
   270	We'll sometimes call $F$ a formula. 
   271	The form of the clause function depends on the type of satisfiability problem we are interested in (for the moment, SAT, NAESAT or XORSAT).
   272	Let $y[V]$ denote an assignment $y_1,\dots,y_n$ of Boolean values to the variables in $V$, and $y[e]$ its restriction to the $k$ variables in $e$.
   273	By $\chi_e(y[e])$ we mean the result of evaluating $\chi_e$ on the $k$ values in some \emph{fixed} order (for the moment, the actual choice of ordering of variables in edges isn't important but it will be when we consider certain planted models in Section \ref{sec:planted}.)
   274	This model naturally captures familiar satisfiability problems:
   275	
   276	\begin{itemize}
   277		\item in $k$-SAT, we have $\chi_e(y[e]) = 1 \iff y[e]\neq x_e$ where $x_e \in \{0,1\}^k$ represents a particular forbidden pattern,
   278		\item in $k$-NAESAT, we have $\chi_e(y[e]) = 1 \iff y[e]\notin \{x_e, \overline{x_e}\}$ where $\overline{x_e}$ is the result of flipping each component of $x_e$,
   279		\item in $k$-XORSAT, we have $\chi_e(y[e]) = 1 \iff \oplus_i y_i = x_e$ where now $x_e\in \{0,1\}$.
   280	\end{itemize}
   281	
   282	% We will assume clause functions take one of these three forms, though some of our results extend to other models as we'll see in Section \ref{sec:planted}.
   283	An assignment which satisfies all clauses in $F$ is called a satisfying assignment (or solution) for $F$.
   284	Let $C_k(n)$ be the set of all possible $k$-clauses on $V$ and write $N = |C_k(n)|$; in $k$-SAT for example we have $N=\binom{n}{k}2^k$.
   285	We use the \emph{binomial model} for a random CSP with clause density $\alpha\in [0,N/n]$, and draw a random formula $G$ as follows:
   286	\footnote{ 
   287	% Note that for $X\subseteq \{0,1\}^N$ we have $\mu_p(X) = \sum_{x\in X}\mu_p(x)$.
   288	One could also consider the \emph{uniform model}, wherein $G(n,\alpha)$ is chosen uniformly from those vectors $x \in \{0,1\}^N$ with $|x| = \alpha n$, where $ {\alpha n}/N = p$.
   289	The models are essentially equivalent, and we mostly focus here on the binomial model. }
   290	
   291	\begin{equation}
   292	\text{ $\bullet$ include in $G$ each clause in $C_k(n)$ with probability $p={\alpha n}/N$.}
   293	\end{equation}
   294	Let $G(n,\alpha)$ denote a formula obtained by this process.
   295	The formula $G(n,\alpha)$ can be viewed as a random element of $\{0,1\}^N$, drawn according to the product measure $\mu_p$.
   296	\footnote{To see the correspondance, identify each of the $N$ components with a clause and set it to $1$ if and only if the clause is present in the formula.}
   297	% Our probability space $\{0,1\}^N$, equipped with a product measure $\mu_p$.
   298	That is, for each $x\in \{0,1\}^N$ we have $\mu_p(x) := \prob[G(n,\alpha) = x] = p^{|x|}(1-p)^{N-|x|}$ (where $|x|$ denotes the number of nonzero components). 
   299	Now consider a procedure to sample a \emph{planted} CSP $F$. 
   300	\begin{align}\label{proc:planted}
   301		&\bullet \text{ Sample $v^0 \in \{0,1\}^n$ uniformly at random.}\\
   302		\nonumber &\bullet \text{ Then include in $F$ each $k$-clause which is satisfied by $v^0$ independently with probability}\\ 
   303		\nonumber &\hspace{15pt} \text{$ p={\alpha n}/N$.}
   304	\end{align}
   305	We use the notation $F(n,\alpha)$ to denote a formula obtained by this process.
   306	% For simplicity, in what follows we restrict our attention to $k$-SAT; the extension to $k$-NAESAT and $k$-XORSAT is easy.
   307	By construction such a formula is always satisfied by the assignment $v_i=v_i^0$; the vector $v^0$ is known as the planted solution.
   308	Let $Z(F)$ denote the cardinality of the set of assignments to $v_1,\dots,v_n$ which satisfy $F$.
   309	Notice that we always have $Z(F(n,\alpha) \geq 1$ by construction.
   310	Again we view $F(n,\alpha)$ as an element of $\{0,1\}^N$ but 
   311	observe that the distribution in this case satisfies $\mu_p(F) = \prob [F(n,\alpha) = F] = \tfrac{Z(F)}{2^n}p^{|F|}(1-p)^{\binom nk (2^k-1) - |F|} $ so $\mu_p$ is not a product measure here.
   312	% We remark that in the limit the number of clauses in $G(n,\alpha)$ or $F(n,\alpha)$ is distributed as a Poisson random variable with mean $\alpha n$ by the standard binomial Poisson approximation.
   313	
   314	
   315	\subsection{CSPs arising from Goldreich's one-way function candidate}\label{sec:goldmodel}
   316	The CSPs introduced in the previous section have clause functions taking a few specific forms.
   317	In these examples a satisfying variable assignment $y[V]$ satisfies $\chi_e(y[e])=1, \forall e$,
   318	and the clause functions on individual edges are independent of one another.
   319	Our concentration results can be extended to a related class of CSPs which are related to Goldreich's proposed one-way function \cite{Go00}.
   320	The idea is that we can consider CSPs with arbitrary clause functions if the clauses on different edges are related in a specific way.
   321	
   322	In \cite{Go00} Goldreich proposed a candidate one-way function family which exploits the difficulty of recovering a solution to a form of planted CSP.
   323	\footnote{One-way functions are important objects in cryptography and complexity theory. Intuitively these are functions that are computationally easy to evaluate, but hard to invert. For a thorough discussion see \cite{Go05}, \cite{Go00}. }
   324	Goldreich's original proposition was that the following function $f$ is one-way.
   325	As always we work with the variable set $V = \{v_1,\dots,v_n\}$.
   326	\begin{itemize}
   327		\item Select a predicate $\chi: \{0,1\}^k \rightarrow \{0,1\}$ uniformly at random from the set of all such Boolean functions.
   328		\item Draw a sparse Erd\H{o}s-R\'{e}nyi $k$-uniform multi-hypergraph $(V,E)$ with $m$ edges $e_1,\dots,e_m$.
   329		\item $f:\{0,1\}^n \rightarrow \{0,1\}^m$ is the function with $f(x)_i = \chi(x[e_i])$, i.e. the $i$th output bit is the result of evaluating $\phi$ on the $k$ (ordered) values assigned to the edge $e_i$.
   330	\end{itemize}
   331	More precisely, Goldreich conjectured that $f$ is one-way in the setting where $k=O(\log n)$ and $m=n$, and the graph is a sufficiently good expander, 
   332	% in the sense that every $d$ edges covers at least $d+ \Omega(n)$ variables, 
   333	for most choices of the predicate $\chi$ which is randomly selected and hard-wired into $f$.
   334	
   335	With this in mind we can define a class of planted CSPs generated by the following procedure.
   336	\begin{align}\label{proc:gold}
   337		\nonumber &\bullet \text{ Select a predicate $\chi: \{0,1\}^k \rightarrow \{0,1\}$.}\\
   338		 &\bullet \text{ Sample $v^0 \in \{0,1\}^n$ uniformly at random.}\\
   339		\nonumber &\bullet \text{ Then include in $F$ each $k$-clause of the form $e$ with $\chi(y[e]) = \chi(v^0[e])$, }\\
   340		\nonumber & \text{ \hspace{5pt} with probability $p={\alpha n}/N$.}
   341	\end{align}
   342	Here the edges are ordered subsets of $V$, and so $N = \binom{n}{k}k!$.
