   387	As an intermediate step toward Theorem \ref{thm:main}, we will prove that for fixed $\phi\in [0,1]$ there is a sharp threshold density for the property of having fewer than $2^{n\phi}$ solutions (we define these terms in Section \ref{sec:bourgain}).
   388	First, in Section \ref{sec:planted} we prove the following $n$-dependent sharp threshold.
   389	
   390	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sharp}
   391	For every $k\geq 2$ and for every $\phi \in [0,1)$ there exists a sequence $\{\alpha_n(\phi) \}_{n \in \mathbb Z_{>0}}$ such that for every $\epsilon>0$,
   392	$$\lim_{n\to \infty} Q_n(\alpha_n(\phi)-\epsilon, \phi) = 0 $$
   393	$$\lim_{n\to \infty} Q_n(\alpha_n(\phi)+\epsilon, \phi) = 1. $$
   394	\end{lemma}
   395	
   396	In fact, we prove Lemma \ref{lem:sharp} for a larger class of planted CSPs, namely those which arise from Goldreich's one-way function candidate \cite{Go00}.
   397	This allows us to deduce the analogous statement of Theorem \ref{thm:main} for certain instances of these CSPs, as well as an $n$-dependent version of it in general.
   398	
   399	In Section \ref{sec:freeze} we combine Lemma \ref{lem:sharp} with Theorem \ref{thm:conv} using a technique from \cite{AbMo13RSA} to show that the sequence $\alpha_n(\phi)$ converges. 
   400	
   401	\begin{theorem}\label{thm:freeze}
   402	For every $k\geq 2$, there exist a countable set $\mathcal C$ and a function $\phi_s:[0,\alpha^*) \to [0,1]$ such that for each $\phi\in \phi_s([0,\infty))$ there exists $\alpha_s(\phi)$ such that for each $\epsilon>0$,
   403	$$\lim_{n\to \infty}  Q_n(\alpha_s(\phi) - \epsilon, \phi) = 0$$
   404	and
   405	$$\lim_{n\to \infty}  Q_n(\alpha_s(\phi) + \epsilon, \phi) = 1$$
   406	\end{theorem}
   407	We deduce Theorem \ref{thm:main} from Theorem \ref{thm:freeze} in Section \ref{sec:freeze}.
   408	
   409	\subsection{Concentration of the number of solutions of CSPs from Goldreich's one-way function candidates}
   410	We now present concentration results for the number of solutions of the CSPs arising from Goldreich's one-way function candidates described in Section \ref{sec:goldmodel}.
   411	
   412	If one considers the logarithm of the number of solutions of the one-way function candidate determined by a random graph $G$, a predicate $\chi$ and a uniform input, and takes the average over the input distribution, it is possible to obtain the following concentration result. 
   413	Note that this gives a stronger concentration notion, i.e., almost sure and for every $\alpha$, and imposes no restriction on the choice of $\chi$. 
   414	However, it provides a $n$-dependent threshold and requires averaging over the input distribution.  
   415	%In fact we may drop it and obtain the following $n$-dependent version of Theorem \ref{thm:main}.
   416	
   417	\begin{lemma}\label{thm:goldreichcurve}
   418	Let $F(n,\alpha)$ be a formula drawn as in \eqref{proc:gold} %(with $\chi$ not necessarily antisymmetric).
   419	Then for every $k\geq 2$, there exist a function $\phi_s^n: [0,\alpha^*] \to [0,1]$, namely $\phi_s^n=\E_{G,v^0} \log Z(F(n,\alpha))$, such that for every $\alpha >0$ and every $\epsilon > 0$, the following holds almost surely 
   420	$$\lim_{n \to \infty} (\E_{v^0} \log Z(F(n,\alpha))-\E_{G,v^0} \log Z(F(n,\alpha))) = 0.$$
   421	%$$\lim_{n\to \infty} Q_n(\alpha, \phi_s^n(\alpha)-\epsilon) = 0 $$
   422	%$$\lim_{n\to \infty} Q_n(\alpha, \phi_s^n(\alpha)+\epsilon) = 1 $$
   423	\end{lemma}
   424	
   425	The proof is found in Appendix Section \ref{sec:goldreichcurve}. 
   426	
   427	We can dispose of the dependence of $\phi_s$ on $n$ and on the averaging of the input in the previous theorem for certain choices of $\chi$.
   428	We simply need to remark that Theorem \ref{thm:conv} was in fact shown in \cite{AbMo13RSA} to hold for planted formulas $F(n,\alpha)$ which satisfy a certain convexity hypothesis (let's call it $H$ for now), then the proof of the following theorem follows that of Theorem \ref{thm:main} exactly as in Section \ref{sec:freeze}.
   429	To this end, in the Appendix Section \ref{sec:sharpgold} we prove the analogue of Lemma \ref{lem:sharp} for these CSPs.
   430	This allows us to deduce the analogous statement of Theorem \ref{thm:main} for certain instances of these CSPs.
   431	
   432	We say that a predicate $\chi: \{0,1\}^k \rightarrow \{0,1\}$ is \emph{balanced} if it evaluates to $1$ on exactly half of the inputs and we say $\chi$ is antisymmetric if $\chi(x) = 1-\chi(\overline{x})$ for some $x \in \{0,1\}^k$.
   433	\begin{theorem}\label{thm:goldreichconvex}
   434	Let $F(n,\alpha)$ be a formula drawn as in \ref{proc:gold}, with a predicate $\chi$ which is antisymmetric and satisfies Hypothesis $H$.
   435	Then for every $k\geq 2$, there exist a countable set $\mathcal D$ and a function $\phi_s: [0,\alpha^*] \to [0,1]$ such that for every $\alpha \notin \mathcal D$ and every $\epsilon > 0$,
   436	$$\lim_{n\to \infty} Q_n(\alpha, \phi_s(\alpha)-\epsilon) = 0 $$
   437	$$\lim_{n\to \infty} Q_n(\alpha, \phi_s(\alpha)+\epsilon) = 1 $$
   438	\end{theorem}
   439	
   440	The hypothesis $H$, stated in terms of $\chi$ is as follows.
   441	
   442	\begin{dfn}\label{def:h-gold}
   443	Let $M_1(\{0,1\}^k)$ denote the space of probability measures on $\{0,1\}^k$. Let $\ell \geq 1$. Define $\Gamma: M_1(\{0,1\}^k) \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ by
   444	$$\nu \mapsto \Gamma_{\ell}(\nu) = \frac{1}{2} \sum_{\substack{u^{(1)}, \dots,u^{(\ell)} \in \{0,1\}^k \\ \chi(u^{(1)}) = \dots = \chi(u^{(\ell)})}}  \prod_{i=1}^k \nu(u_i^{(1)},\dots,u_i^{(\ell)})$$
   445	\end{dfn}
   446	
   447	\begin{hyp}
   448	For each $\ell \geq 1$, the operator $\Gamma$ is convex in $\nu$.
   449	\end{hyp}
   450	 
   451	Bogdanov and Qiao showed in \cite{BoQi09} that for many choices of $\chi$, Goldreich's function can be inverted with high probability when $m$ is larger than $n$ by a sufficiently large constant factor.
   452	In particular any $\chi$ which is not balanced or whose output correlates with one or two bits of the input is a bad choice when $m=Dn$ for sufficiently large constant $D$.
   453	Their result suggests that if we want the resulting function to be one-way then we may want $\chi$ to be balanced and not correlated with any bit or pair of bits of the input, but it is unclear whether these would be necessary in the regime $m=n$, the one Goldreich originally suggested.
   454	
   455	Strictly speaking, the restriction to antisymmetric $\chi$ in Theorem \ref{thm:goldreichconvex} does not seem necessary.
   456	It is a technical condition which arises in the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:sharp}.
   457	We have verified using a computer search that when $k\leq 5$ no antisymmetric function satisfies the balance properties along with Hypothesis $H$ but it remains unclear to us whether such a function can exist in general.
   458	
